1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,951
Zoya found her customer: busy parents.

2
00:00:02,951 --> 00:00:06,017
So she made them a big
jar of eighteen laddoos.

3
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:10,327
Three hundred and sixty rupees,
sold only from Nani's kitchen.

4
00:00:10,677 --> 00:00:11,577
Mrs.

5
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:12,963
Rao never heard of it.

6
00:00:12,963 --> 00:00:16,360
And a jar does not fit
in a tiffin box anyway.

7
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:18,276
The customer was right.

8
00:00:18,276 --> 00:00:19,604
The mix was wrong.

9
00:00:20,254 --> 00:00:22,469
So the team sits down to plan again.

10
00:00:22,819 --> 00:00:24,543
Nani guards the recipe.

11
00:00:24,543 --> 00:00:26,319
Arjun knows the streets.

12
00:00:26,319 --> 00:00:27,990
Dev makes the posters.

13
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:30,198
And Zoya asks four questions.

14
00:00:30,198 --> 00:00:31,397
What do we sell?

15
00:00:31,397 --> 00:00:32,400
For how much?

16
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:33,300
Where?

17
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:34,880
And how will people find out?

18
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:38,387
Those four questions make
up the marketing mix.

19
00:00:38,687 --> 00:00:43,158
Kotler and Armstrong call it the set
of marketing tools a company blends,

20
00:00:43,158 --> 00:00:46,526
to get the response it wants
from its target market.

21
00:00:46,876 --> 00:00:48,037
Think of a cook.

22
00:00:48,037 --> 00:00:52,583
The same ingredients, mixed well or
badly, make a great dish or a bad one.

23
00:00:52,933 --> 00:00:58,032
Neil Borden of Harvard made the
phrase famous in a 1953 speech.

24
00:00:58,032 --> 00:01:00,434
He listed twelve ingredients.

25
00:01:00,784 --> 00:01:05,243
In 1960, Jerome McCarthy grouped
them into four simple Ps.

26
00:01:05,243 --> 00:01:08,196
Product, price, place and promotion.

27
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:10,031
Let's run Mrs.

28
00:01:10,031 --> 00:01:11,380
Rao's week twice.

29
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,124
First, with the jar.

30
00:01:13,474 --> 00:01:14,374
Promotion.

31
00:01:14,325 --> 00:01:16,215
Nobody told her about it.

32
00:01:16,215 --> 00:01:17,885
She stops right there.

33
00:01:17,885 --> 00:01:18,785
No sale.

34
00:01:19,044 --> 00:01:23,495
And even if she had heard, the jar
was hard to find, too costly, and too

35
00:01:23,495 --> 00:01:24,710
big for a tiffin.

36
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:26,655
Now, the new mix.

37
00:01:26,655 --> 00:01:30,540
Dev puts posters at the school
gate, and parents get free tastes.

38
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:31,790
Place.

39
00:01:31,518 --> 00:01:34,475
Arjun delivers to her
building every Monday.

40
00:01:34,825 --> 00:01:35,725
Price.

41
00:01:35,441 --> 00:01:37,936
A hundred and twenty rupees for six.

42
00:01:37,936 --> 00:01:40,219
That's twenty rupees a school day.

43
00:01:40,569 --> 00:01:41,469
Product.

44
00:01:41,245 --> 00:01:45,309
Six small laddoos, each one
wrapped, made to fit a tiffin box.

45
00:01:45,659 --> 00:01:46,935
Every gate opens.

46
00:01:46,935 --> 00:01:49,705
She buys, and she buys again next week.

47
00:01:50,355 --> 00:01:52,567
One weak P can sink the whole mix.

48
00:01:53,267 --> 00:01:54,846
Here is the full mix.

49
00:01:54,846 --> 00:01:56,771
Each P answers one question.

50
00:01:57,121 --> 00:01:58,021
Product.

51
00:01:57,835 --> 00:01:59,134
What do we sell?

52
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:01,610
The six-pack, with no refined sugar.

53
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:02,810
Price.

54
00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:03,762
What do they pay?

55
00:02:03,762 --> 00:02:05,965
A fair price for a healthy snack.

56
00:02:06,265 --> 00:02:07,165
Place.

57
00:02:06,795 --> 00:02:08,183
Where do they get it?

58
00:02:08,183 --> 00:02:10,500
At their door, before the school week.

59
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,700
Promotion.

60
00:02:11,598 --> 00:02:13,174
How do they find out?

61
00:02:13,174 --> 00:02:16,394
Posters, free tastes, and
happy parents talking.

62
00:02:16,744 --> 00:02:19,047
And all four point at the same customer.

63
00:02:19,047 --> 00:02:21,797
That is what makes it
a mix, not just a list.

64
00:02:22,447 --> 00:02:25,080
Where do we see the 4Ps at work today?

65
00:02:25,430 --> 00:02:26,330
Product.

66
00:02:26,216 --> 00:02:29,582
A phone maker sells one
model in three sizes.

67
00:02:29,932 --> 00:02:30,832
Price.

68
00:02:30,576 --> 00:02:33,392
A cinema charges less for morning shows.

69
00:02:33,742 --> 00:02:34,642
Place.

70
00:02:34,285 --> 00:02:37,313
A bakery sells from its
shop, and through an app.

71
00:02:37,663 --> 00:02:38,563
Promotion.

72
00:02:38,452 --> 00:02:41,162
A hospital runs a free health check camp.

73
00:02:41,812 --> 00:02:43,990
But the 4Ps have critics too.

74
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:45,521
The good side.

75
00:02:45,521 --> 00:02:49,159
Four simple boxes, so nothing
important is forgotten.

76
00:02:49,509 --> 00:02:50,538
The hard side.

77
00:02:50,538 --> 00:02:55,027
They look from the seller's side, and
they fit products better than services.

78
00:02:55,377 --> 00:03:00,916
In 1990, Robert Lauterborn turned them
into four Cs, seen from the buyer's

79
00:03:00,916 --> 00:03:01,816
side.

80
00:03:01,929 --> 00:03:04,078
Product becomes customer needs.

81
00:03:04,078 --> 00:03:05,531
Price becomes cost.

82
00:03:05,531 --> 00:03:07,354
Place becomes convenience.

83
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:09,492
Promotion becomes communication.

84
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:10,742
The lesson?

85
00:03:10,709 --> 00:03:14,536
Plan with the Ps, then check
them through the customer's eyes.

86
00:03:15,186 --> 00:03:16,257
Quick check.

87
00:03:16,257 --> 00:03:18,806
Arjun delivers the six-pack to Mrs.

88
00:03:18,806 --> 00:03:20,836
Rao's building every Monday.

89
00:03:20,836 --> 00:03:22,259
Which P is this?

90
00:03:24,159 --> 00:03:25,059
Place!

91
00:03:24,739 --> 00:03:28,262
It is about where and how the
customer gets the product.

92
00:03:28,862 --> 00:03:29,871
So, remember.

93
00:03:30,171 --> 00:03:34,664
The marketing mix is the set of tools
you control: product, price, place

94
00:03:34,664 --> 00:03:35,680
and promotion.

95
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:40,346
Each P answers one question: what,
how much, where, and how people find

96
00:03:40,346 --> 00:03:41,246
out.

97
00:03:41,156 --> 00:03:43,749
And all four must point
at the same customer.

98
00:03:44,349 --> 00:03:49,383
Next time, we'll add three more Ps for
services: people, process and physical

99
00:03:49,383 --> 00:03:50,283
evidence.
